¶
'''astronomian†''' (as-trọ̄-nō′mi-a̤n), ''n.'' [< ME. ''astronomien'', ''astronomyen'', < OF. ''astronomien'' = Pr. ''astronomian'', < ML. as if *''astronomianus'', < L. ''astronomia'': see ''astronomy'' and ''-an''.] An astronomer; any one having knowledge of the stars.
{{smaller block|''Astronomians'' came from the East. {{right|''Wyclif'', Mat. ii. 1.}}}}

'''astronomic''' (as-trọ̄-nom′ik), ''a.'' [= F. ''astronomique'', < L. ''astronomicus'', < Gr. ἀστρονομικός, < ἀστρονομία, astronomy.] Of or pertaining to astronomy: as, ''astronomic'' facts.

'''astronomical''' (as-trọ̄-nom′i-kạl), ''a.'' [< ''astronomic'' + ''-al''.] Pertaining or related to astronomy; connected with or relating to astronomic observation or research.—'''Astronomical chronology.''' {{smaller|See ''chronology''.}}—'''Astronomical clock,''' {{smaller|a clock which keeps sidereal time.}}—'''Astronomical column, day, horizon,''' {{smaller|etc. See the nouns.}}—'''Astronomical lantern,''' {{smaller|a lamp having a glass or paper screen on which a celestial map is drawn.}}—'''Astronomical signs,''' {{smaller|the signs of the zodiac.}}—'''Astronomical year.''' {{smaller|See ''year''.}}

'''astronomically''' (as-trọ̄-nom′i-kạl-i), ''adv.'' In an astronomical manner; by means of astronomy, or according to astronomic principles or methods.

'''astronomicon†''' (as-trọ̄-nom′i-kon), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀστρονομικόν, neut. of ἀστρονομικός: see ''astronomic''.] A treatise on the stars.

'''astronomics''' (as-trọ̄-nom′iks), ''n.'' [Pl. of ''astronomic'': see ''-ics''.] Astronomy.
{{smaller block|The laws of Gravitation, Statics, Acoustics, Chemics, Optics, Pneumatics, Magnetics, ''Astronomics'' . . . are all reducible to numerical language.
{{right|''G. D. Boardman'', Creative Week, p. 310, App.}}}}

